1
00:00:04,900 --> 00:00:08,082
[SPEAKER_01]: I guess the question is, what needs to be restrained?

2
00:00:10,323 --> 00:00:11,944
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, do you say no to everything?

3
00:00:11,984 --> 00:00:13,565
[SPEAKER_01]: To whatever your eye wants to see?

4
00:00:14,526 --> 00:00:16,026
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever your nose wants to smell?

5
00:00:18,668 --> 00:00:20,069
[SPEAKER_01]: How would you know what to say no to?

6
00:00:20,089 --> 00:00:34,357
[SPEAKER_00]: Depends on why I want to do that thing.

7
00:00:36,389 --> 00:00:36,669
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah?

8
00:00:37,549 --> 00:00:41,951
[SPEAKER_00]: Depends on what my motivation is.

9
00:00:43,331 --> 00:00:47,232
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what would then be the motivation that you should restrain things out of?

10
00:00:49,853 --> 00:00:53,554
[SPEAKER_00]: If I want pleasure.

11
00:00:54,794 --> 00:00:55,835
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you always want pleasure.

12
00:00:56,375 --> 00:00:57,335
[SPEAKER_01]: That's your starting point.

13
00:00:57,675 --> 00:00:58,796
[SPEAKER_00]: If I don't want pain.

14
00:00:59,976 --> 00:01:00,976
[SPEAKER_01]: You always don't want pain.

15
00:01:01,436 --> 00:01:03,137
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the implicit starting point as well.

16
00:01:06,704 --> 00:01:07,345
[SPEAKER_00]: I want to be...

17
00:01:07,605 --> 00:01:09,687
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whatever you do, it's done for the sake of pleasure.

18
00:01:10,528 --> 00:01:18,875
[SPEAKER_01]: But, see, you then, trying to say no to every desire, to everything, will also be done for the sake of pleasure.

19
00:01:19,716 --> 00:01:22,339
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you'll be denying everything for the same reason.

20
00:01:22,459 --> 00:01:23,660
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it won't take you out of it.

21
00:01:27,023 --> 00:01:32,008
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's... that's the... the... the sutta, the simile of the... of the deer herd, the bait.

22
00:01:33,685 --> 00:01:39,950
[SPEAKER_01]: First people, first group of deer, they were just eating carelessly, and they got hooked onto the bait.

23
00:01:40,611 --> 00:01:48,577
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, the second group was... saw that, but then dismissed everything, all the food, and ran into the mountains.

24
00:01:48,617 --> 00:01:52,581
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, they starved there, had to come back, ate carelessly.

25
00:01:52,621 --> 00:01:59,666
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, the herd that the Buddha praised is the one that sort of realizes that, and it stays within the bait, but doesn't eat that which is a bait.

26
00:01:59,947 --> 00:02:01,388
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it eats carefully around the bait.

27
00:02:04,507 --> 00:02:17,792
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the reason I'm bringing this up is because, like, you know, the talk we had about devaluing sensuality, and, again, if people are not, well, I guess, experienced, or sufficiently experienced, they might fall into, like, oh, I must now deny everything.

28
00:02:17,812 --> 00:02:18,532
[SPEAKER_01]: I want to say this, no.

29
00:02:18,732 --> 00:02:19,352
[SPEAKER_01]: I want to hear this, no.

30
00:02:19,372 --> 00:02:20,673
[SPEAKER_01]: I want to taste this, no.

31
00:02:21,473 --> 00:02:22,774
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, why are you saying no to everything?

32
00:02:22,794 --> 00:02:23,734
[SPEAKER_01]: To have context on that?

33
00:02:23,894 --> 00:02:24,354
[SPEAKER_01]: No, you don't.

34
00:02:25,935 --> 00:02:31,357
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the sole purpose of restraint is to develop context in regard to your own motivation, in regard to your own desires.

35
00:02:32,233 --> 00:02:36,796
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to understand that you saying... you wanting to practice restraint is rooted in the same desires.

36
00:02:37,277 --> 00:02:42,080
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what would have meant desires to be abandoned by the means of desire, craving to be abandoned by the means of craving.

37
00:02:42,701 --> 00:02:48,625
[SPEAKER_01]: But not simply desire can be abandoned through the desire, or craving can be abandoned through craving.

38
00:02:48,965 --> 00:02:50,607
[SPEAKER_01]: Just by craving you'll abandon craving.

39
00:02:50,767 --> 00:02:50,927
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

40
00:02:52,905 --> 00:03:12,240
[SPEAKER_01]: You crave for craving, for freedom from craving initially, but if you start looking at the context of your motivation, the... where things are rooted in, what type of intention is behind what you want to do, what you've done, what you're planning to do, that's then how you basically make craving redundant.

41
00:03:14,528 --> 00:03:17,749
[SPEAKER_01]: By avoiding doing things that perpetuate craving.

42
00:03:18,089 --> 00:03:19,910
[SPEAKER_01]: Because not all things perpetuate craving.

43
00:03:20,470 --> 00:03:22,891
[SPEAKER_01]: Not all things perpetuate ignorance.

44
00:03:22,931 --> 00:03:28,212
[SPEAKER_01]: Things that are rooted in lust, aversion and distraction perpetuate craving, ignorance and everything unwholesome.

45
00:03:29,033 --> 00:03:33,814
[SPEAKER_01]: But see, lust, aversion and distraction are not in things that you're doing.

46
00:03:33,874 --> 00:03:37,175
[SPEAKER_01]: They're in your motivation that makes you do the thing for those reasons.

47
00:03:39,598 --> 00:03:45,082
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you want to find lust, aversion, delusion, it's not simply by doing this and abstaining from that.

48
00:03:46,183 --> 00:03:51,227
[SPEAKER_01]: It's basically by understanding whether you are doing it for the sake of lust, aversion or distraction.

49
00:03:53,208 --> 00:03:57,632
[SPEAKER_01]: So, none of those... none of the lust, aversion, distraction can be in things, not even in central objects.

50
00:03:58,693 --> 00:03:59,533
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what Buddha said.

51
00:03:59,934 --> 00:04:08,180
[SPEAKER_01]: But the target, the lust, desire and lust have been removed from his mind, so the beautiful objects just remain standing there in the world, beautiful, nothing else.

52
00:04:10,172 --> 00:04:19,917
[SPEAKER_01]: But for that, you need to first restrain your habitual action towards beautiful, habitual action away from the ugly, habitual action distract yourself from neutral boredom or whatever else.

53
00:04:21,057 --> 00:04:22,838
[SPEAKER_01]: But not for its own sake.

54
00:04:22,898 --> 00:04:30,382
[SPEAKER_01]: It's only for the sake to see how lust, aversion, delusion are actually on the level of your intentions, your choices, not in those things.

55
00:04:33,733 --> 00:04:39,080
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's also like... we covered that in other talks, but that's also how virtue and duty is affected.

56
00:04:39,140 --> 00:04:45,389
[SPEAKER_01]: It's because it's on the level of you assuming lust, aversion, delusion to be in these things that you're now abstaining from.

57
00:04:46,630 --> 00:04:49,274
[SPEAKER_01]: And by abstaining from it, you think that's it, that's all you need to do.

58
00:04:51,908 --> 00:05:04,215
[SPEAKER_01]: If you think that your mission is accomplished by abstaining from bad things, you will not look for where the badness of bad things is really rooted, which is in your motivation for keeping the precepts or for breaking them.

59
00:05:08,798 --> 00:05:14,861
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when we talk about the sensory strain, yeah, you take on the precepts, five precepts, but ideally you want to take eight precepts.

60
00:05:15,873 --> 00:05:19,615
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, within that, you see what mind presents you with.

61
00:05:19,675 --> 00:05:21,395
[SPEAKER_01]: Things to do throughout... You have to do... Say...

62
00:05:21,635 --> 00:05:26,777
[SPEAKER_01]: If you say, I will not do anything throughout the day, because everything is rooted in desire, that will be rooted in desire.

63
00:05:27,178 --> 00:05:27,878
[SPEAKER_01]: That's your desire.

64
00:05:28,338 --> 00:05:31,799
[SPEAKER_01]: If you say, I should stop thinking, I will not think about anything, that is your thought.

65
00:05:33,260 --> 00:05:36,101
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't just decide and step outside of the problem.

66
00:05:36,361 --> 00:05:37,061
[SPEAKER_01]: You are the problem.

67
00:05:37,081 --> 00:05:38,202
[SPEAKER_01]: Your existence is the problem.

68
00:05:40,133 --> 00:05:49,940
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you take on the precepts, you take on sense restraint on a level of faith first, and now you go throughout your day having to do things.

69
00:05:50,720 --> 00:05:52,722
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can't stop doing, and you shouldn't stop doing.

70
00:05:54,243 --> 00:05:58,826
[SPEAKER_01]: You just want to know where the motivation for you doing those things is rooted.

71
00:05:59,807 --> 00:06:02,568
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you wonder, okay, so how far I go with this?

72
00:06:02,649 --> 00:06:04,250
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you go within the precepts.

73
00:06:04,979 --> 00:06:12,330
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if things that are presented to you to be done throughout the day would be breaking the precepts, you just say no to that.

74
00:06:12,430 --> 00:06:14,473
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need to think too much about that side of things.

75
00:06:14,593 --> 00:06:16,496
[SPEAKER_01]: No, this is against the precepts that I've taken on.

76
00:06:17,443 --> 00:06:22,328
[SPEAKER_01]: But if it's within the precepts, it doesn't mean, oh, I can do it justifiably, because it's not breaking the precepts.

77
00:06:23,209 --> 00:06:26,792
[SPEAKER_01]: Nor is it, I must deny everything, because everything is rooted in desire.

78
00:06:26,932 --> 00:06:27,052
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

79
00:06:27,353 --> 00:06:28,213
[SPEAKER_01]: You look at the time.

80
00:06:28,253 --> 00:06:32,297
[SPEAKER_01]: The same thing today can be rooted in one thing, one motivation.

81
00:06:32,317 --> 00:06:33,959
[SPEAKER_01]: Tomorrow, it can be rooted in another motivation.

82
00:06:34,419 --> 00:06:39,444
[SPEAKER_01]: But if only you see is the thing that you say either always yes to or no to, you're never going to see your motivation.

83
00:06:39,765 --> 00:06:42,067
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's where the whole problem is, always has been.

84
00:06:43,245 --> 00:06:49,010
[SPEAKER_01]: The lust, aversion, delusion can never step outside of the domain of your intention and enter these things in the world.

85
00:06:49,270 --> 00:06:50,832
[SPEAKER_01]: No, not at all, not the slightest.

86
00:06:52,513 --> 00:06:54,195
[SPEAKER_01]: Always remains within your intention.

87
00:06:54,215 --> 00:07:04,423
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you can recognize lust, for example, you can recognize... Well, first you need to recognize your intention, your motivation, then you see if it's lustful, if it's aversive, or if it's sort of wanting to distract itself.

88
00:07:04,684 --> 00:07:08,147
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, in the background, so to speak, you're always watching out for that.

89
00:07:08,627 --> 00:07:08,907
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

90
00:07:08,947 --> 00:07:16,590
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the more effort you make towards discerning it, well, the clearer the discernment will become.

91
00:07:16,650 --> 00:07:19,811
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means then, you don't need to almost stop and figure this out.

92
00:07:19,951 --> 00:07:23,792
[SPEAKER_01]: It's all just... basically, the flags will be there already, because you developed them.

93
00:07:24,592 --> 00:07:29,874
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even if you're not directly thinking about your motivation, you already know, oh, this is a lustful mind.

94
00:07:29,994 --> 00:07:31,094
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the satipaṭṭhāna.

95
00:07:31,134 --> 00:07:32,915
[SPEAKER_01]: He knows lustful mind is lustful mind.

96
00:07:33,960 --> 00:07:35,861
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not lustful thing in front of him.

97
00:07:36,001 --> 00:07:36,161
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

98
00:07:36,261 --> 00:07:40,743
[SPEAKER_01]: The mind, the intent, that's what basically citta, cetanā, relationship.

99
00:07:41,163 --> 00:07:44,945
[SPEAKER_01]: Intention and intent, in a general sense, it's what mind is.

100
00:07:45,065 --> 00:07:46,706
[SPEAKER_01]: Already the direction of some kind.

101
00:07:46,726 --> 00:07:49,467
[SPEAKER_00]: It's always... there is some intention always.

102
00:07:49,547 --> 00:07:52,508
[SPEAKER_01]: There is always a mind there, which means there is always an intent there.

103
00:07:52,628 --> 00:07:53,168
[SPEAKER_00]: So, being able to...

104
00:07:53,228 --> 00:07:59,290
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means it's always... the intent always has to be either lust or non-lust, aversion or non-aversion, distraction or non-distraction.

105
00:07:59,771 --> 00:08:09,754
[SPEAKER_01]: But, if all you see is things you do, things you don't do, and then just meditation technique on top of it, or whatever else, you're never looking at where the problem actually is, and you're never going to see it.

106
00:08:09,974 --> 00:08:13,036
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you assume it to be in things you're saying no to.

107
00:08:14,116 --> 00:08:17,037
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why... well... oh, so the problem is in the desire.

108
00:08:17,797 --> 00:08:19,358
[SPEAKER_01]: Desire towards this or towards that.

109
00:08:20,435 --> 00:08:21,356
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I'll say no to that.

110
00:08:21,436 --> 00:08:23,978
[SPEAKER_01]: But now I have a desire to say no to everything.

111
00:08:24,018 --> 00:08:24,959
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I'll say no to that.

112
00:08:25,499 --> 00:08:26,019
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, now I have...

113
00:08:26,200 --> 00:08:27,100
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I'll say no to that.

114
00:08:27,200 --> 00:08:35,967
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you just start saying no to more and more things and wind yourself up completely and equally have no perspective in as much as if you were engaging with sensuality.

115
00:08:37,088 --> 00:08:42,192
[SPEAKER_01]: That's like the ignoble way of that self-mortification and denial of everything.

116
00:08:42,212 --> 00:08:44,574
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, you have the precepts within that.

117
00:08:45,795 --> 00:08:46,556
[SPEAKER_01]: You question things.

118
00:08:46,876 --> 00:08:51,379
[SPEAKER_01]: Look, I want to go for a walk, as we say often, as a kind of a neutral example.

119
00:08:52,440 --> 00:08:52,640
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

120
00:08:53,080 --> 00:08:55,322
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, because I'm restless a bit here, now?

121
00:08:56,783 --> 00:08:57,063
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

122
00:08:57,583 --> 00:08:59,244
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's not the perfect motivation.

123
00:08:59,825 --> 00:09:01,086
[SPEAKER_01]: But is it central motivation?

124
00:09:01,126 --> 00:09:03,768
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it because I'm hoping to see, sight, sound, smell, taste, touch it?

125
00:09:04,248 --> 00:09:05,729
[SPEAKER_01]: They will excite me.

126
00:09:05,749 --> 00:09:06,610
[SPEAKER_01]: No, not specifically.

127
00:09:06,650 --> 00:09:10,532
[SPEAKER_01]: Just because it's too hot under this roof and I want some breeze.

128
00:09:11,613 --> 00:09:12,774
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, that's not... that's all right.

129
00:09:14,313 --> 00:09:21,600
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not sure how ideal it is or not, but I know that it's not as bad as like sensuality, as breaking the precepts.

130
00:09:22,020 --> 00:09:22,561
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you do it.

131
00:09:24,983 --> 00:09:28,866
[SPEAKER_01]: You go for a walk because you want some breeze, you want some comfort.

132
00:09:30,728 --> 00:09:35,132
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, next time, I want to go for a walk, still same comfort.

133
00:09:35,192 --> 00:09:36,614
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, but now again, you re-evaluate.

134
00:09:37,014 --> 00:09:39,016
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't now just do it because you've done it before.

135
00:09:40,388 --> 00:09:41,670
[SPEAKER_01]: Also, is it still rooted in comfort?

136
00:09:41,710 --> 00:09:41,891
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

137
00:09:42,351 --> 00:09:46,118
[SPEAKER_01]: Still not rooted in sensuality of the, you know, exciting kind?

138
00:09:46,378 --> 00:09:46,578
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

139
00:09:47,380 --> 00:09:50,505
[SPEAKER_01]: But can I perhaps endure the discomfort and not go for a walk?

140
00:09:52,708 --> 00:09:54,632
[SPEAKER_01]: Just, if you have strength, to ask yourself.

141
00:09:55,904 --> 00:10:03,467
[SPEAKER_01]: You provide that direction, or maybe saying no to that, but you do not always say no to it, or always say yes to it, because once you decided to... to do so.

142
00:10:04,187 --> 00:10:06,488
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you always want to see where your motivation is rooted.

143
00:10:07,148 --> 00:10:09,309
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, why do I want the breeze and the comfort?

144
00:10:09,349 --> 00:10:18,212
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, because actually I'm sleepy, and I'll fall asleep, but if I go for a walk, I'll be awake, and then I can spend more time practicing mindfulness and walk meditation or reading the suttas.

145
00:10:19,133 --> 00:10:20,213
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, well, that's a good reason now.

146
00:10:21,304 --> 00:10:22,224
[SPEAKER_01]: Same, like with the Buddha.

147
00:10:22,644 --> 00:10:25,665
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, what if jhāna that I attained in the past is the way?

148
00:10:26,665 --> 00:10:32,067
[SPEAKER_01]: I think it is the way, but I can't now develop it, because I'm too hungry, I'm starving, I'm in too much pain.

149
00:10:32,767 --> 00:10:38,108
[SPEAKER_01]: So let me eat this this rice, whatever the lady offered him, get some strength, so that I can do it.

150
00:10:40,089 --> 00:10:43,830
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah, that's a valuable motivation, that's a reasonable, that's a good motivation.

151
00:10:44,690 --> 00:10:46,670
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, no, I just want to go for a walk, because I'm bored.

152
00:10:47,511 --> 00:10:47,991
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not it.

153
00:10:48,171 --> 00:10:48,791
[SPEAKER_01]: Why are you bored?

154
00:10:49,742 --> 00:10:51,683
[SPEAKER_01]: Why do you have motivation to get rid of the border?

155
00:10:52,644 --> 00:10:54,265
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean, like...

156
00:10:54,305 --> 00:10:59,409
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't want, when we talk about the sensory strain, people to jump into conclusion that you must say no to everything, because that's equally ignoble.

157
00:10:59,769 --> 00:11:03,372
[SPEAKER_01]: But it doesn't mean, oh, by not saying no to everything means I can break the precepts sometimes.

158
00:11:03,432 --> 00:11:04,592
[SPEAKER_01]: No, that's even worse.

159
00:11:06,934 --> 00:11:08,715
[SPEAKER_01]: Sensuality is worse than asceticism.

160
00:11:09,496 --> 00:11:15,380
[SPEAKER_01]: But asceticism will not take you out of the... will not uproot your craving and desire in itself either.

161
00:11:16,947 --> 00:11:18,628
[SPEAKER_01]: The first response is giving carelessly.

162
00:11:19,188 --> 00:11:20,809
[SPEAKER_01]: Second response is saying no to everything.

163
00:11:21,530 --> 00:11:23,891
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you go back to stuff you should have given up.

164
00:11:24,411 --> 00:11:31,275
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, only the response in regard to that, in that simile of the deer herd, is like, oh, wait.

165
00:11:31,835 --> 00:11:37,578
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if I stay within this, but I don't cross certain boundaries, and within that boundary, I discern the motivation.

166
00:11:39,368 --> 00:11:46,497
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, yeah, you will know exactly when things are wholesome, when things are unwholesome, even before they come anywhere near the border of breaking the precept.

167
00:11:48,900 --> 00:11:51,243
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're always maintaining a perspective.

168
00:11:54,304 --> 00:11:58,045
[SPEAKER_01]: You maintain the perspective on your own intent.

169
00:11:58,225 --> 00:12:02,087
[SPEAKER_01]: Every situation is already a type of intent.

170
00:12:02,127 --> 00:12:04,147
[SPEAKER_01]: I intend to sit here and continue talking to you.

171
00:12:04,888 --> 00:12:05,528
[SPEAKER_01]: Where is that intent?

172
00:12:05,608 --> 00:12:07,168
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, so let me ask myself now.

173
00:12:07,869 --> 00:12:10,370
[SPEAKER_01]: Where is that intent of mine to continue?

174
00:12:10,410 --> 00:12:10,770
[SPEAKER_01]: Root it.

175
00:12:11,570 --> 00:12:13,271
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the end game of this, so to speak?

176
00:12:14,071 --> 00:12:15,612
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, it's recording the dhamma talk.

177
00:12:15,772 --> 00:12:16,492
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, that's fine.

178
00:12:17,455 --> 00:12:24,199
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's the thing, that you need to know how to have that perspective, to be able to see your mind, your intentions... And you will get to know it... while you're doing things.

179
00:12:24,299 --> 00:12:29,782
[SPEAKER_01]: You will get to know that perspective by first practicing, keeping the precepts, and practicing sense restraint.

180
00:12:30,302 --> 00:12:34,665
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, and trying to... By creating the boundary within which you can then discern.

181
00:12:34,845 --> 00:12:44,010
[SPEAKER_01]: You can allow your mind to move left to right, within the boundary, not over the boundary, and then you can see, ah, when it moves to left, it's rooted in this, when it moves to right, it's rooted in that.

182
00:12:44,370 --> 00:12:46,932
[SPEAKER_00]: To say, keeping physically

183
00:12:50,799 --> 00:12:53,019
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, no.

184
00:12:54,059 --> 00:12:57,560
[SPEAKER_01]: Knowing what you need to discern will help you discern on the basis of precepts.

185
00:12:58,860 --> 00:13:01,741
[SPEAKER_01]: Automatically keeping the precepts will not make you discern.

186
00:13:01,881 --> 00:13:05,861
[SPEAKER_01]: It will just make you keep the precepts for whatever assumed value you have.

187
00:13:06,101 --> 00:13:07,922
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's a necessary basis.

188
00:13:08,462 --> 00:13:09,282
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not optional.

189
00:13:09,482 --> 00:13:13,543
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot understand without having kept the precepts beforehand, as the Buddha would say.

190
00:13:14,915 --> 00:13:23,121
[SPEAKER_01]: So, knowing that you're not supposed to stop thinking and watch the nostrils and just be mindless and adhere to this.

191
00:13:23,701 --> 00:13:28,565
[SPEAKER_01]: Knowing that you are supposed to think about what is the intent behind me doing what I'm doing right now.

192
00:13:30,466 --> 00:13:35,750
[SPEAKER_01]: Knowing that you're not supposed to stop doing everything, say no to everything, get rid of everything.

193
00:13:36,534 --> 00:13:44,139
[SPEAKER_01]: But instead, understand where things that are being done, or things you intend to do, where are those things rooted in?

194
00:13:44,959 --> 00:13:47,621
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the background of this intention I want to do?

195
00:13:47,901 --> 00:13:52,024
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there's a lustful intention.

196
00:13:52,724 --> 00:13:53,225
[SPEAKER_00]: You see that?

197
00:13:53,545 --> 00:13:54,506
[SPEAKER_00]: There's a lustful intention.

198
00:13:54,546 --> 00:13:57,287
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's not going to be like, oh, there's a lustful intention floating in my mind.

199
00:13:57,347 --> 00:13:58,929
[SPEAKER_01]: It's going to start with something specific.

200
00:14:00,229 --> 00:14:02,651
[SPEAKER_01]: I heard people talking there.

201
00:14:03,151 --> 00:14:04,072
[SPEAKER_01]: I want to go and see.

202
00:14:05,413 --> 00:14:05,633
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

203
00:14:07,041 --> 00:14:10,363
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the intent behind your action to go and see who's talking there?

204
00:14:11,484 --> 00:14:12,625
[SPEAKER_01]: It can be a million things.

205
00:14:12,665 --> 00:14:17,328
[SPEAKER_01]: It can be, oh, I don't know, the thieves or hunters came to hunt animals here, so I want to chase them off.

206
00:14:18,009 --> 00:14:21,832
[SPEAKER_01]: Or it can be, oh, maybe it's just some people that will be entertaining to talk to.

207
00:14:22,882 --> 00:14:25,464
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah, so if it's that, where is that intention rooted?

208
00:14:25,804 --> 00:14:32,068
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's rooted in me wanting basically excitement, wanting to be distracted from this neutral feeling, pretty much.

209
00:14:32,129 --> 00:14:39,674
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm neither in pain nor in pleasure, but the offer of excitement of meeting other people or something, oh, that's that's exhilarating to me.

210
00:14:40,174 --> 00:14:41,415
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's clearly unwholesome.

211
00:14:41,775 --> 00:14:43,156
[SPEAKER_01]: So then you say no to that.

212
00:14:43,977 --> 00:14:44,837
[SPEAKER_01]: You endure that.

213
00:14:44,917 --> 00:14:47,639
[SPEAKER_01]: But if it's on another occasion, oh, so I will never go and talk to people now.

214
00:14:48,880 --> 00:14:50,802
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it depends on the motivation it's rooted in.

215
00:14:51,898 --> 00:14:56,283
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whatever you're about to do, you must question.

216
00:14:56,924 --> 00:15:03,191
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever you're about to do, whatever you are in the middle of doing, if you lose context of why it's done, you remind yourself.

217
00:15:03,591 --> 00:15:05,333
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you... it's not a mystery.

218
00:15:06,134 --> 00:15:11,220
[SPEAKER_01]: The only reason you're still doing these things or intending to do those things is because your intent is there as a necessary basis.

219
00:15:12,438 --> 00:15:13,759
[SPEAKER_01]: Simultaneously enduring with it.

220
00:15:13,939 --> 00:15:21,586
[SPEAKER_00]: Just want to become familiar without having... familiar with your intentions, without having going through the whole thinking process over and over again.

221
00:15:21,626 --> 00:15:23,087
[SPEAKER_00]: So, why am I moving my hand?

222
00:15:23,208 --> 00:15:24,549
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly, exactly.

223
00:15:24,889 --> 00:15:33,236
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to see the intent, not go through the motions of, basically, thoughts that you've done in the past, where you kind of got the sense of the intent.

224
00:15:33,981 --> 00:15:37,043
[SPEAKER_00]: But let's say, like, now, I want to get up.

225
00:15:37,284 --> 00:15:37,724
[SPEAKER_00]: So I don't...

226
00:15:37,984 --> 00:15:38,625
[SPEAKER_01]: I must not get up.

227
00:15:38,685 --> 00:15:38,825
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

228
00:15:38,965 --> 00:15:39,585
[SPEAKER_01]: You might get up.

229
00:15:39,645 --> 00:15:40,246
[SPEAKER_01]: You might not get up.

230
00:15:40,266 --> 00:15:41,547
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't matter.

231
00:15:41,887 --> 00:15:43,028
[SPEAKER_01]: If you want to get up, you get up.

232
00:15:43,488 --> 00:15:44,609
[SPEAKER_01]: If you don't want to get up, you don't get up.

233
00:15:44,889 --> 00:15:47,511
[SPEAKER_01]: But you want to know, like, yeah, but what's my intent?

234
00:15:48,052 --> 00:15:49,873
[SPEAKER_01]: Why do I want to get up?

235
00:15:49,893 --> 00:15:54,277
[SPEAKER_01]: That does not... See, when you say the question, why do I want to get up?

236
00:15:54,677 --> 00:15:58,920
[SPEAKER_01]: People can sometimes immediately jump into the conclusion, ah, getting up is bad, I must not get up.

237
00:15:59,000 --> 00:15:59,161
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

238
00:15:59,761 --> 00:16:00,442
[SPEAKER_01]: You just want to

239
00:16:04,812 --> 00:16:05,392
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even if it's...

240
00:16:05,412 --> 00:16:07,653
[SPEAKER_01]: I want to get up because of the desire for more comfort.

241
00:16:07,753 --> 00:16:08,013
[SPEAKER_01]: Fine.

242
00:16:08,533 --> 00:16:10,694
[SPEAKER_01]: There is nothing sensual.

243
00:16:11,034 --> 00:16:11,855
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no ill-will.

244
00:16:11,895 --> 00:16:12,855
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no sexuality.

245
00:16:12,935 --> 00:16:13,835
[SPEAKER_01]: We're just getting up.

246
00:16:14,816 --> 00:16:18,797
[SPEAKER_01]: It's still a desire, inasmuch as when you want to deny everything.

247
00:16:18,837 --> 00:16:21,178
[SPEAKER_01]: So, everything is a desire.

248
00:16:21,698 --> 00:16:23,759
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you want to get up, you get up.

249
00:16:23,999 --> 00:16:25,599
[SPEAKER_00]: But I've got to think about it before.

250
00:16:26,240 --> 00:16:28,280
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't even need to think about it before, necessarily.

251
00:16:28,821 --> 00:16:30,141
[SPEAKER_01]: You might do it in the beginning.

252
00:16:30,681 --> 00:16:32,482
[SPEAKER_01]: You might have to think about it in the beginning.

253
00:16:33,232 --> 00:16:37,273
[SPEAKER_01]: But, once you get to know that, okay, this is not against the precepts to get up.

254
00:16:37,373 --> 00:16:38,553
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, so, why do I get up?

255
00:16:38,673 --> 00:16:39,013
[SPEAKER_01]: Comfort.

256
00:16:39,273 --> 00:16:40,193
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm getting up for comfort.

257
00:16:40,233 --> 00:16:40,513
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

258
00:16:40,933 --> 00:16:42,274
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the next time, why do I get comfort?

259
00:16:42,734 --> 00:16:44,054
[SPEAKER_01]: So, next time, because of comfort.

260
00:16:44,114 --> 00:16:47,815
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you're going to have a question, inevitably asked, like, naturally.

261
00:16:48,695 --> 00:16:51,876
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, so, do I need to go throughout my day just doing things out of comfort?

262
00:16:52,376 --> 00:16:55,236
[SPEAKER_01]: So, next time, the context has now been upgraded.

263
00:16:55,276 --> 00:16:57,097
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you want to get up out of comfort.

264
00:16:57,877 --> 00:16:58,957
[SPEAKER_01]: Can I endure this comfort?

265
00:16:59,527 --> 00:17:01,391
[SPEAKER_01]: Now you will know, why would I endure discomfort?

266
00:17:01,471 --> 00:17:03,815
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like, I will not get up, I will endure the pain.

267
00:17:03,975 --> 00:17:07,422
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but now you have to ask a question in regard to that, because that's now the thing you're doing.

268
00:17:08,504 --> 00:17:10,607
[SPEAKER_01]: Why do I want to endure discomfort?

269
00:17:11,400 --> 00:17:19,402
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what it would have meant, like, when monks would... some monks were asking him to make these uncomfortable practices mandatory, because they're good.

270
00:17:19,882 --> 00:17:20,662
[SPEAKER_01]: And he said, no.

271
00:17:20,722 --> 00:17:24,203
[SPEAKER_01]: Let those who want to do those things, do them, and those who don't want to, not do them.

272
00:17:24,803 --> 00:17:26,203
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it depends where it's rooted at.

273
00:17:26,363 --> 00:17:30,484
[SPEAKER_01]: For somebody, you... enduring discomfort can be utterly, completely unwholesome.

274
00:17:31,444 --> 00:17:35,565
[SPEAKER_01]: And always rooted unwholesome, but they will only know that if they get to discern their intent.

275
00:17:37,239 --> 00:17:40,840
[SPEAKER_01]: So, now you don't want to get up, because you want to challenge yourself with a bit of discomfort.

276
00:17:41,181 --> 00:17:41,421
[SPEAKER_01]: Fine.

277
00:17:41,701 --> 00:17:41,921
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

278
00:17:42,761 --> 00:17:46,503
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you see relationship between the challenge of discomfort and gaining perspective?

279
00:17:47,023 --> 00:17:49,824
[SPEAKER_01]: Or are you just doing it out of a reaction to your comfort?

280
00:17:52,885 --> 00:17:56,167
[SPEAKER_01]: Either way, you can do either way, because it's not against the precepts.

281
00:17:56,926 --> 00:18:01,588
[SPEAKER_01]: It's getting up from comfort or discomfort, but you want to know what is your intent behind.

282
00:18:02,729 --> 00:18:07,371
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is the process of learning how to discern your intention?

283
00:18:08,232 --> 00:18:09,933
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's what I'm describing now.

284
00:18:10,013 --> 00:18:10,573
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

285
00:18:11,333 --> 00:18:18,137
[SPEAKER_00]: So, before I act, I must question my intentions.

286
00:18:18,197 --> 00:18:18,997
[SPEAKER_00]: Before I act.

287
00:18:19,217 --> 00:18:20,838
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

288
00:18:21,058 --> 00:18:24,600
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if I, before I do anything,

289
00:18:26,025 --> 00:18:34,251
[SPEAKER_00]: I have to think why am I doing this, why am I doing this, because this, because that, because that... and then decide... Well, you might have to think that initially, if you're unaware of your intent.

290
00:18:34,611 --> 00:18:34,731
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

291
00:18:34,971 --> 00:18:38,994
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you start doing it rightly, you won't even need to verbally ask your questions.

292
00:18:39,014 --> 00:18:41,296
[SPEAKER_01]: You will already see the intent that's implicit.

293
00:18:42,568 --> 00:18:44,289
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, impossibility of this action.

294
00:18:44,469 --> 00:18:47,850
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you've discerned lust for intention.

295
00:18:48,230 --> 00:18:56,494
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when it comes again, when you're doing that, an action that's going to be based on lust or intention, you recognize it immediately.

296
00:18:56,514 --> 00:18:57,414
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you see the framework of lust.

297
00:18:57,454 --> 00:18:58,374
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, immediately it's seen.

298
00:18:58,835 --> 00:18:59,495
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, this is lust.

299
00:18:59,555 --> 00:19:00,996
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't have to go through that whole process.

300
00:19:01,036 --> 00:19:01,476
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no.

301
00:19:01,676 --> 00:19:02,596
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it lust?

302
00:19:03,777 --> 00:19:04,897
[SPEAKER_00]: Why am I saying it's lust?

303
00:19:05,477 --> 00:19:06,238
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, see, that's the thing.

304
00:19:06,258 --> 00:19:11,420
[SPEAKER_01]: When people will inevitably fall into that mechanical repetition of doing the same questions,

305
00:19:12,394 --> 00:19:17,896
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, they fail to see that, that now, there is an intent in regard to that, and they're not seeing it.

306
00:19:19,697 --> 00:19:21,758
[SPEAKER_01]: So, first time, you ask yourself, why do I want to get up?

307
00:19:21,818 --> 00:19:22,318
[SPEAKER_01]: Why do I want to...?

308
00:19:22,558 --> 00:19:25,099
[SPEAKER_01]: Because of this... And then, you arrive at the conclusion, it's because of the lust.

309
00:19:26,220 --> 00:19:28,381
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, next time, you immediately...

310
00:19:28,401 --> 00:19:32,822
[SPEAKER_01]: But now, you don't actually direct your mind towards intent, and then use the questions to reveal the intent.

311
00:19:33,323 --> 00:19:38,965
[SPEAKER_01]: You now direct your minds towards the questioning you've done before, hoping you will arrive at the same knowledge of the same intent.

312
00:19:39,805 --> 00:19:41,766
[SPEAKER_01]: And now you're doing it mechanically.

313
00:19:41,806 --> 00:19:47,969
[SPEAKER_01]: But now, all you need to do is, at any given time, just... Oh, but what is my intent behind this questioning?

314
00:19:48,669 --> 00:19:53,691
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why am I questioning now my motivation in regard to wanting to get up?

315
00:19:53,711 --> 00:19:56,792
[SPEAKER_01]: Do I really don't know whether it's done for comfort or discomfort?

316
00:19:57,173 --> 00:20:04,016
[SPEAKER_01]: Or am I doing it out of this compulsion to go through these motions that revealed it the first time, and now I feel obliged, dutiful to repeat?

317
00:20:05,596 --> 00:20:05,716
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh!

318
00:20:06,477 --> 00:20:07,617
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I already know my intent.

319
00:20:07,897 --> 00:20:09,538
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I don't need to ask these questions at all now.

320
00:20:10,380 --> 00:20:13,563
[SPEAKER_01]: Next time, I'm not sure, and then questions will naturally arise.

321
00:20:14,323 --> 00:20:16,225
[SPEAKER_01]: But sometimes, intent is much clearer.

322
00:20:17,266 --> 00:20:23,431
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you just adhere now, that you always must do this questioning, you're actually obscuring your intent by the questioning.

323
00:20:26,593 --> 00:20:28,215
[SPEAKER_01]: Either way, you will get confused.

324
00:20:28,535 --> 00:20:31,858
[SPEAKER_01]: The point is, you don't step over the boundary of breaking the precepts.

325
00:20:33,046 --> 00:20:36,969
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when the confusion is dispelled, you're still held within the same container.

326
00:20:37,810 --> 00:20:42,193
[SPEAKER_01]: You, when you restart, you restart already with more experience and more recognition and more discernment.

327
00:20:42,854 --> 00:20:46,496
[SPEAKER_01]: But if your confusion takes you outside of the precepts, now you need to rebuild it.

328
00:20:47,978 --> 00:20:49,038
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why they're not optional.

329
00:20:50,940 --> 00:20:55,043
[SPEAKER_01]: And, yeah, you can do the same practice within the five precepts, but it's going to be much more watered down.

330
00:20:56,064 --> 00:21:00,067
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, if you want to practice the dhamma on the level that it matches, it can basically

331
00:21:02,206 --> 00:21:05,389
[SPEAKER_01]: To make you gain the right view, you need to do the eight precepts.

332
00:21:06,710 --> 00:21:09,172
[SPEAKER_01]: But within there, again, it's not like no to everything.

333
00:21:09,533 --> 00:21:09,913
[SPEAKER_01]: Not at all.

334
00:21:10,353 --> 00:21:12,035
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's exactly what the Buddha was doing.

335
00:21:12,335 --> 00:21:17,259
[SPEAKER_01]: What if I were to say no to all the thoughts that are rooted in unwholesome?

336
00:21:18,681 --> 00:21:23,385
[SPEAKER_01]: And what if I were to say yes to all the thoughts that are rooted in wholesome?

337
00:21:23,865 --> 00:21:25,086
[SPEAKER_01]: Absence of greed, aversion, delusion.

338
00:21:25,427 --> 00:21:25,527
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah!

339
00:21:26,688 --> 00:21:28,890
[SPEAKER_01]: And then the tireless energy arose in him.

340
00:21:29,780 --> 00:21:30,300
[SPEAKER_01]: Day and night.

341
00:21:30,861 --> 00:21:33,002
[SPEAKER_01]: Because he was just avoiding unwholesome, thinking wholesome.

342
00:21:33,803 --> 00:21:36,965
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, obviously, that was the Buddha, so he could do that on the level of thoughts.

343
00:21:37,005 --> 00:21:40,268
[SPEAKER_01]: Means he didn't even have to worry about a speech and action, because that's already within that.

344
00:21:40,868 --> 00:21:55,158
[SPEAKER_01]: But, today's mind, when you start, you have to start first from your actions, then come to your speech, and then, eventually, if you cultivate that long enough, accurately enough, you will be able to say, I have these thoughts appeared in my mind.

345
00:21:55,599 --> 00:21:56,239
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing to do with...

346
00:21:56,259 --> 00:21:57,000
[SPEAKER_01]: I didn't intend them.

347
00:21:57,775 --> 00:22:01,058
[SPEAKER_01]: But, sensual thoughts, non-sensual thoughts, you will know right away.

348
00:22:01,619 --> 00:22:04,602
[SPEAKER_01]: Sensual thoughts, I don't delight, I don't welcome, I don't value.

349
00:22:06,264 --> 00:22:08,366
[SPEAKER_01]: Non-sensual thoughts, fine, they can stay.

350
00:22:08,806 --> 00:22:10,328
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the mind has to think.

351
00:22:10,368 --> 00:22:12,530
[SPEAKER_01]: If you say, I will not think this, well, you're thinking that.

352
00:22:13,591 --> 00:22:19,878
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you think that by avoiding to think, you're somehow stepping outside of the domain of thought, well, you're basically self-deluding yourself.

353
00:22:23,490 --> 00:22:31,299
[SPEAKER_01]: So, within the precepts, it's not about saying no to everything, saying yes to everything, or saying no to things you said no to, saying yes to things you said yes to automatically.

354
00:22:31,959 --> 00:22:35,243
[SPEAKER_01]: It's about discerning the intent behind your current situation.

355
00:22:37,904 --> 00:22:39,344
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, now I need to go and do such and such.

356
00:22:39,704 --> 00:22:40,805
[SPEAKER_01]: What's my intent behind that?

357
00:22:40,925 --> 00:22:42,105
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, it's some practical purpose.

358
00:22:42,145 --> 00:22:42,845
[SPEAKER_01]: It needs to be finished.

359
00:22:43,285 --> 00:22:43,905
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, that's fine.

360
00:22:44,065 --> 00:22:46,406
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't even need to... Yeah, but what if, what if, what if?

361
00:22:46,966 --> 00:22:52,427
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, if you get into that compulsive, what if this is wholesome, this is unwholesome, what's the intent behind that compulsion?

362
00:22:53,087 --> 00:22:55,808
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, the intent is to get rid of the pain of compulsion.

363
00:22:56,508 --> 00:22:57,728
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the intent is aversion.

364
00:22:58,408 --> 00:22:59,589
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the intent is unwholesome.

365
00:23:00,369 --> 00:23:05,290
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I'm wholesomely questioning my intent for the unwholesome purpose of getting rid

366
00:23:07,705 --> 00:23:08,625
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is not wholesome.

367
00:23:09,846 --> 00:23:17,188
[SPEAKER_01]: And you will fall into that confusion, or you will not learn how to step out of that confusion, if you just keep giving into it.

368
00:23:18,669 --> 00:23:28,712
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you... if you basically go... start doing the questioning of your intent mechanically, because you depend, oh, it's easier to just mindlessly do this, this, this, this, the result will come.

369
00:23:29,372 --> 00:23:29,512
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

370
00:23:29,612 --> 00:23:31,793
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the intent behind me wanting that result?

371
00:23:33,710 --> 00:23:36,833
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's... once you actually start doing it, rightly, this whole space opens up.

372
00:23:37,654 --> 00:23:39,917
[SPEAKER_01]: Millions of things you can do, for right reasons.

373
00:23:41,458 --> 00:23:44,902
[SPEAKER_01]: And becomes much less constricted than in the beginning, when it's just...

374
00:23:44,922 --> 00:23:45,863
[SPEAKER_01]: I must say no to everything.

375
00:23:45,903 --> 00:23:46,704
[SPEAKER_01]: I must say no to everything.

376
00:23:46,744 --> 00:23:47,304
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a desire.

377
00:23:47,324 --> 00:23:48,125
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything is a desire.

378
00:23:49,126 --> 00:23:51,509
[SPEAKER_01]: But, yeah, you kind of have to start like that to some extent.

379
00:23:53,487 --> 00:23:54,368
[SPEAKER_01]: It simplifies the work.

380
00:23:54,408 --> 00:23:54,668
[SPEAKER_01]: It does.

381
00:23:55,208 --> 00:23:55,469
[SPEAKER_01]: It does.

382
00:23:55,489 --> 00:24:04,395
[SPEAKER_01]: In the beginning, and also, as I said, like the simile of the deer herd, once you realize, ah, it's only things that are rooted in greed, it's only this is the bait.

383
00:24:04,656 --> 00:24:06,477
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything else, I don't have to worry about.

384
00:24:06,497 --> 00:24:07,858
[SPEAKER_01]: This is not essential pleasure.

385
00:24:09,319 --> 00:24:11,100
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't need to fear it, as the Buddha would say.

386
00:24:13,342 --> 00:24:22,249
[SPEAKER_01]: But only once you accept it, okay, I need to know the intent behind acting out of my feeling, acting towards pleasure, acting out of pain.

387
00:24:23,819 --> 00:24:26,040
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's where lust, aversion, delusion is rooted.

388
00:24:26,100 --> 00:24:27,240
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not in the feeling.

389
00:24:27,300 --> 00:24:29,321
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not in the object of your senses.

390
00:24:29,361 --> 00:24:33,463
[SPEAKER_01]: It's rooted in you being unaware of your intent.

391
00:24:33,643 --> 00:24:35,944
[SPEAKER_01]: Ignorance of the intent is what the problem already is.

392
00:24:37,744 --> 00:24:44,487
[SPEAKER_01]: So, once you become discerning of the intent, you simply avoid the one that's rooted in lust, aversion, delusion.

393
00:24:44,587 --> 00:24:46,848
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how lust, aversion, delusion will wither away.

394
00:24:47,731 --> 00:24:48,872
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they need maintenance.

395
00:24:49,352 --> 00:24:52,095
[SPEAKER_01]: Maintenance through ignorance of what those things are.

396
00:24:52,495 --> 00:24:54,136
[SPEAKER_01]: That you don't even know that you're maintaining it.

397
00:24:55,117 --> 00:25:03,885
[SPEAKER_01]: But now that you know that this is what things rooted in lust, intent of lust are, this is what intent of lust is, you don't do that intent of lust.

398
00:25:04,045 --> 00:25:04,886
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't act out of it.

399
00:25:05,586 --> 00:25:08,729
[SPEAKER_01]: When intent of lust changes, it's intent of non-lust, you can do that.

400
00:25:09,309 --> 00:25:10,711
[SPEAKER_01]: It might be the same thing externally.

401
00:25:10,851 --> 00:25:13,453
[SPEAKER_01]: Going for a walk, meeting people to talk to,

402
00:25:18,892 --> 00:25:26,100
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, the lust and aversion, delusion, distraction, will have to diminish, wear off, exactly.

403
00:25:42,885 --> 00:25:48,927
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, this is just for the purpose of people who take on precepts, sense-restraint.

404
00:25:49,527 --> 00:25:51,448
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't do sense-restraint mechanically either.

405
00:25:52,028 --> 00:26:02,311
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, as I said, if it's within the precepts, the same thing, sometimes you need to restrain it, sometimes you don't need to, depending on the intent, not depending on the thing that you're restraining to.

406
00:26:03,452 --> 00:26:05,332
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I will never look at anything agreeable.

407
00:26:05,933 --> 00:26:06,733
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but why is that?

408
00:26:08,874 --> 00:26:10,114
[SPEAKER_01]: Why do you have that intent now?

409
00:26:12,157 --> 00:26:14,839
[SPEAKER_01]: So, do you blame the agreeable for your suffering?

410
00:26:17,861 --> 00:26:18,581
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yes, I do.

411
00:26:18,682 --> 00:26:19,742
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's a mistake, isn't it?

412
00:26:19,822 --> 00:26:22,244
[SPEAKER_01]: Because suffering is not in the agreeable nor disagreeable.

413
00:26:24,305 --> 00:26:27,287
[SPEAKER_01]: Suffering is in you either resisting it or lustfully going towards it.

414
00:26:28,288 --> 00:26:29,549
[SPEAKER_01]: So, suffering is in your intent.

415
00:26:31,190 --> 00:26:40,997
[SPEAKER_01]: So, knowing the intent on account of agreeable or disagreeable within the precepts, yeah, that's how your mind will have to develop right now.

416
00:26:43,722 --> 00:26:50,328
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you get confused about what's my intent, just step back and see what's your intent in regard to that.

417
00:26:51,448 --> 00:26:54,051
[SPEAKER_01]: And that would be your intent, the current situation intent.

418
00:26:56,132 --> 00:26:59,996
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's why there is no method, or motions, or sets of questions you always ask.

419
00:27:00,076 --> 00:27:00,256
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

420
00:27:01,297 --> 00:27:05,160
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes you need to drill further, longer, because the intent is more obscure.

421
00:27:06,109 --> 00:27:13,013
[SPEAKER_01]: But more often than not, the intent is right there, because the intent is the basis for the context for anything you're about to do, you're doing.

422
00:27:13,033 --> 00:27:23,659
[SPEAKER_01]: See, like, my intent to go and talk to those people over there, I don't need to now think about getting off the chair, opening the gate, walking left, right, left, right.

423
00:27:24,019 --> 00:27:30,003
[SPEAKER_01]: All of that is unified, and I'm doing it mindlessly, because it's kept together by the intent to meet those people down there.

424
00:27:32,595 --> 00:27:33,757
[SPEAKER_01]: If suddenly my intent...

425
00:27:33,777 --> 00:27:34,679
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't want to meet those people.

426
00:27:35,040 --> 00:27:35,961
[SPEAKER_01]: I want to go on top of the hill.

427
00:27:38,486 --> 00:27:42,293
[SPEAKER_01]: I will stop going in that direction and stop doing actions that will lead me to that intent.

428
00:27:42,753 --> 00:27:43,976
[SPEAKER_01]: So the intent is always there.

429
00:27:45,724 --> 00:27:49,647
[SPEAKER_01]: If you catch yourself in doubt, you realize, oh, actually, that's why doubt is a hindrance.

430
00:27:50,268 --> 00:27:52,289
[SPEAKER_01]: Because doubt covers up what you already know.

431
00:27:52,470 --> 00:28:03,939
[SPEAKER_01]: You already know the intent, but now the doubt is this compulsion that presses you to go through these motions and try to, seemingly, clarify intent, while the real purpose is just get rid of the pain of the doubt.

432
00:28:04,780 --> 00:28:11,105
[SPEAKER_01]: All you need to do in order to get rid of the pain of the doubt is stop trying to get rid of the pain of the doubt.

433
00:28:11,125 --> 00:28:12,326
[SPEAKER_01]: Stop acting out of aversion.

434
00:28:12,646 --> 00:28:13,687
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

435
00:28:13,727 --> 00:28:14,888
[SPEAKER_01]: The doubt will have to subside.

436
00:28:21,091 --> 00:28:21,571
[SPEAKER_01]: And you know that.

437
00:28:21,592 --> 00:28:22,332
[SPEAKER_01]: You know the difference.

438
00:28:22,352 --> 00:28:25,035
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, oh, I'm questioning my intent, like this.

439
00:28:26,916 --> 00:28:32,301
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm arriving at a conclusion, but then it's sort of... you overdo that and now it becomes emotional.

440
00:28:32,361 --> 00:28:37,046
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, I have this compulsion to keep questioning, to keep repeating the question, keep finding the same answer.

441
00:28:37,646 --> 00:28:39,107
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, now you're just acting out of compulsion.

442
00:28:39,147 --> 00:28:40,289
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not looking for the intent.

443
00:28:40,349 --> 00:28:44,973
[SPEAKER_01]: You're just trying to get rid of the discomfort, which means you're completely unaware of that intent.

444
00:28:52,708 --> 00:29:00,896
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's how... oh, I don't need to fear this type of pleasure, or I don't need to keep, you know, having ill will towards this type of discomfort.

445
00:29:01,817 --> 00:29:08,643
[SPEAKER_01]: You can actually... you become relaxed within the precepts, within sense restraint, because now you know the motivation, always.

446
00:29:09,384 --> 00:29:11,906
[SPEAKER_01]: The intent is there, visible, clearly.

447
00:29:12,267 --> 00:29:15,370
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like knowing the mind, knowing... knowing...

448
00:29:16,763 --> 00:29:18,244
[SPEAKER_01]: Lustful mind is lustful mind.

449
00:29:18,505 --> 00:29:19,806
[SPEAKER_01]: Non-lustful is non-lustful.

450
00:29:19,906 --> 00:29:20,866
[SPEAKER_01]: Elated is elated.

451
00:29:20,887 --> 00:29:21,767
[SPEAKER_01]: Depressed is depressed.

452
00:29:22,248 --> 00:29:24,109
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, it's there, as a container, as a context.

453
00:29:25,550 --> 00:29:28,333
[SPEAKER_01]: Neither pleasant nor unpleasant.

454
00:29:28,373 --> 00:29:31,895
[SPEAKER_01]: And above hindrances.

455
00:29:31,915 --> 00:29:33,056
[SPEAKER_00]: And not acting out of it.

456
00:29:33,277 --> 00:29:33,617
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

457
00:29:34,518 --> 00:29:37,040
[SPEAKER_00]: Not just... not just... oh, that is lustful.

458
00:29:37,820 --> 00:29:41,824
[SPEAKER_00]: As long as I know my lustful intentions, I can still act out of it.

459
00:29:41,844 --> 00:29:42,264
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no.

460
00:29:42,284 --> 00:29:42,624
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the thing.

461
00:29:42,684 --> 00:29:44,306
[SPEAKER_01]: If you know the lust is lust, you don't act.

462
00:29:44,466 --> 00:29:45,587
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the effort you put in.

463
00:29:46,965 --> 00:29:48,187
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't act out of lustful mind.

464
00:29:48,207 --> 00:29:50,750
[SPEAKER_00]: To develop that perspective, recognize, discern...

465
00:29:50,770 --> 00:29:55,317
[SPEAKER_01]: The clearer the perspective is, the easier will be to not act out of it.

466
00:29:55,397 --> 00:29:59,823
[SPEAKER_01]: People act out of habitually, addictively, because there is no perspective on their situation.

467
00:30:00,983 --> 00:30:06,886
[SPEAKER_01]: Once the perspective is increased through your own effort of reflection, oh, now sense restraint is self-evident.

468
00:30:07,006 --> 00:30:07,406
[SPEAKER_01]: I will not...

469
00:30:07,766 --> 00:30:08,226
[SPEAKER_01]: So that...

470
00:30:08,286 --> 00:30:12,548
[SPEAKER_01]: So you restrain only the things that are rooted in lust, aversion, delusion on the level of intent.

471
00:30:12,608 --> 00:30:17,030
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't restrain every sight, every sound, every smell, because that's like starvation.

472
00:30:18,031 --> 00:30:21,552
[SPEAKER_01]: That is literally you run to the top of a mountain when there is no food, and you die.

473
00:30:24,854 --> 00:30:29,856
[SPEAKER_01]: So in order to know then what you need to restrain only, you then need to know the intent.

474
00:30:31,441 --> 00:30:33,663
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's where these things are rooted and need to be restrained.

475
00:30:34,183 --> 00:30:38,486
[SPEAKER_01]: But, yeah, initially you might need to be restraining more than usual to be on a safer side.

476
00:30:38,606 --> 00:30:44,391
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the Buddha would always lean onto the side of asceticism, even if in itself does not necessarily result in wisdom.

477
00:30:45,727 --> 00:30:47,888
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, between the two monks, one will eat, one won't eat.

478
00:30:48,388 --> 00:30:51,129
[SPEAKER_01]: One will eat so he can practice, one say he won't eat so he can practice.

479
00:30:51,209 --> 00:30:53,809
[SPEAKER_01]: I praise the one who won't eat in order to practice.

480
00:30:54,430 --> 00:31:00,991
[SPEAKER_01]: Just in case, lean a bit on the... but not to become neurotic and say no to everything, because that means, oh, you lose context on that.

481
00:31:01,211 --> 00:31:03,032
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't... you're not aware of your own intent there.

